<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Arginusae</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/406-bce">406 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Arginusae was a naval battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a>, fought in 406 BCE near Canae in the Arginusae Islands, east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesbos">Lesbos</a>, in which an Athenian fleet under eight strategoi defeated a Spartan fleet under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/callicratidas">Callicratidas</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> had built and manned the fleet in haste to relieve <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conon">Conon</a>, blockaded at Mytilene, enlisting slaves and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metic">metics</a>; facing more experienced crews, the generals fought in eight divisions and a double line. Callicratidas was killed, and the Spartans lost some 70 ships to the Athenians' 25. A storm kept the ships from rescuing the survivors of the disabled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trireme">triremes</a>, many sailors drowned, and the Athenian assembly tried and executed six of the generals as a group.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-arginusae.jpg" alt="Battle of Arginusae" width="330" height="264" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: A. du Sein (colors added) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arginuses-color.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In 406 BCE Callicratidas replaced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysander">Lysander</a> as navarch of the Spartan fleet. A traditionalist, he distrusted Persian influence and would not ask the Persian prince Cyrus for help, so he raised a fleet of about 140 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trireme">triremes</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>'s Greek allies. After storming Methymna on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesbos">Lesbos</a> he swelled his force to 170 ships and attacked the Athenian commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conon">Conon</a>, who fled to Mytilene and was blockaded there after losing 30 ships. Conon slipped a messenger ship to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>.</p><p>The assembly approved extreme measures: the golden statues of Nike were melted down to pay for ships, and slaves and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metic">metics</a> were enlisted as crews. Contributions from allies raised the fleet to 150 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trireme">triremes</a>, commanded by eight generals acting together.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The Athenians camped at the Arginusae islands, opposite Cape Malea on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesbos">Lesbos</a>. Callicratidas saw their signal fires but a thunderstorm kept him from attacking at night. At dawn he came out with 140 ships and left 50 to watch <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conon">Conon</a>. For the first time in the war the Spartan crews were more experienced than the Athenian, whose best crews were with Conon.</p><p>The Athenians therefore divided into eight autonomous divisions and formed a double line to prevent the diekplous, in which a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trireme">trireme</a> drove through a gap to strike another in the side. They extended their left flank out to sea.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/callicratidas">Callicratidas</a>'s helmsman advised retreat but he pressed on, dividing his force in two. He was killed when his ship rammed an enemy ship, and the Spartan right collapsed; the left soon followed. The Spartans lost some 70 ships and the Athenians 25.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The generals planned to send most of the fleet against the Spartans at Mytilene while Thrasybulus and Theramenes rescued the survivors of the 25 lost ships. A storm thwarted both, and many sailors drowned. When anger rose at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, the generals blamed the two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trierarchy">trierarchs</a>, but these cleared themselves and the public turned on the generals. Two fled; the other six came back.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socrates">Socrates</a>, epistates of the assembly that day, refused to put the motion to a vote, saying he would do nothing contrary to the law, but the motion to try them together carried and all six were executed, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pericles">Pericles</a> the Younger among them. The Athenians soon regretted it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> rejected a Spartan offer of peace, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysander">Lysander</a> won at Aegospotami less than a year later.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Arginusae” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 406 BCE, after the naval victory at <b>Arginusae</b>, the Assembly condemned the victorious generals in a single vote, against the law, for failing to rescue the crews of sinking ships; the six who had returned to Athens were executed, and the Athenians soon regretted it.</blockquote><a href="/government/direct-democracy">Direct democracy · Weaknesses</a></li><li><blockquote>Owners could free their slaves, often in return for the slave’s own savings, and a freed slave became a metic with duties to the former owner; the slaves who rowed at the <b>Battle of Arginusae</b> in 406 BCE were freed and may have been made citizens.</blockquote><a href="/societies/athens">Social Hierarchy of Classical Athens</a></li></ul></section>
